  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 153 AM EDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery and radar returns show showers and thunderstorms \nover northern Minnesota progressively making their way towards the \nU.P. early this morning as a dying cold front pushes eastward. With \nthe LLJ losing steam (as well as the forcing along the front) and \nthe front moving through during mainly the morning to early \nafternoon hours across the U.P., no severe weather is really \nexpected. That being said, there may be a stronger cell or two that \ngets squeaked out as bulk shear is still around 30 to 35 kts along \nthe front. The better, albeit much more conditional, chances for \nsevere weather come behind the front this afternoon as clearing \nskies allow CAPE in the atmosphere to recover while 0-6 km bulk \nshear values range from 30 to potentially even 50 kts. However, with \nthere really being no synoptic forcing mechanism to 'punch' \nconvection up, showers and storms will have to rely on mesoscale \nprocesses (like the lake breeze) to get anything going. Thus, SPC \nkeeps a Marginal (5%) Risk for severe weather over us for hail and \nwind today into early this evening. \n\nMost guidance is now suggesting that convection will once again move \ninto the area sometime on Sunday (save for the NAM). While a \nshortwave impulse is most likely moving into the region on Sunday, \ngiven that it's pretty weak, the convective potential will have to \nbe made-up by the amount of CAPE in the atmosphere. Given that \nconditions will be very warm and somewhat muggy (high potentially in \nthe lower 90s with dewpoints potentially around 70), by the \nafternoon hours 1-3 kJ of SBCAPE is expected to develop; therefore, \nthere will be more than enough energy to get convection going, even \nif the forcing is somewhat lackluster. With the model hodographs \nhaving a veering profile with height, 0-3 km helicity between 100 \nand 300 m2/s2, and 0-6 km bulk shears of 45 to 60 kts, all of the \nsevere threats would theoretically be on the table, the greatest \nbeing severe hail and winds. However, given that the CAMs are \nstruggling still on the exact timing and placement of the \nconvection, chances for convection over any one spot remain low (30% \nor so) across the U.P.. That being said, the western half does have \nslightly better chances of seeing severe weather given the warmer \ntemperatures over there and potentially higher SBCAPE. Thankfully, \nthe severe weather threat drops off after the sun sets Sunday \nevening. \n\nA stronger Clipper low is expected to move through the U.P. on \nMonday, bringing with it additional rain shower and thunderstorm \nchances. Because guidance is suggesting that (generally speaking) \nthe warm sector of the low will be over us during the morning hours, \nthe severe weather threat will be significantly lowered due to the \nlack of diurnal heating creating CAPE; thus the better severe \nweather chances on Monday will be down in the Lower Peninsula. That \nbeing said, with up to around 1500 J of MUCAPE and 30 to 50 kts of \nbulk shear to work with, a low-end severe storm or two cannot be \ncompletely ruled out, even though some capping will need to be \novercome above the boundary layer. \n\nExpect generally quieter weather as we head into the rest of next \nweek. With heights rising over the central CONUS during the mid-week \nbefore moving eastward heading towards the end of the week, we \nshould see at least one day where we remain rain-free (most likely \nTuesday or Wednesday). However, after the center of the high moves \nthrough, positively tilted flow over central Canada and the Northern \nPlains could set us up for additional Clipper shortwaves rotating \ninto the area to end the forecast period.",
